{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab9f3823b9e58d92", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tHighlights\n2\tSpeaker Role and Language Diarization for Analyzing Multilingual Interviews for Language\n3\tProficiency of Older Adults\n4\tAnfeng Xu, Tiantian Feng, Kevin Huang, Pranali Khobragade, Sudarsana Kadiri, Anushikha Dhankhar, Madeleine\n5\tSnider, Sarah Gao, Miguel Arce Rentería, Jinkook Lee, Shrikanth Narayanan\n6\t• We curate speech data from interviews with older adults in India.\n7\t• We manually annotate the speech interviews dataset.\n8\t• We develop Whisper-based speaker-role and language diarization systems.\n9\t• Speech participation and intended-language use relate to language proficiency.\n10\t\n11\tarXiv:2608.09032v1 [eess.AS] 10 Aug 2026\n12\t\n13\t• Automatic diarization yields proficiency predictions comparable to manual regions.\n14\t\n15\t\fSpeaker Role and Language Diarization for Analyzing Multilingual\n16\tInterviews for Language Proficiency of Older Adults⋆\n17\tAnfeng Xua,∗ , Tiantian Fenga , Kevin Huanga , Pranali Khobragadeb , Sudarsana Kadiria ,\n18\tAnushikha Dhankharb , Madeleine Sniderc , Sarah Gaob , Miguel Arce Renteríac , Jinkook Leeb\n19\tand Shrikanth Narayanana\n20\ta Viterbi School of Engineering, University of Southern California, 3740 McClintock Ave, Los Angeles, 90089, CA, USA\n21\tb Center for Economic and Social Research, University of Southern California, 635 Downey Way, Los Angeles, 90007, CA, USA\n22\tc Columbia University Irving Medical Center, 622 W 168th St, New York, 10032, NY, USA\n23\t\n24\tARTICLE INFO\n25\t\n26\tABSTRACT\n27\t\n28\tKeywords:\n29\tspeaker diarization\n30\tlanguage diarization\n31\tlanguage proficiency assessment\n32\tWhisper\n33\tspeech foundation model\n34\t\n35\tAutomatic language proficiency assessment in the context of multilingual interview-based settings remains underexplored. In this work, we develop Whisper-based speaker-role and language\n36\tdiarization systems to automatically extract respondent speech and characterize language usage\n37\tin multilingual interviews with older adults. We further investigate whether diarization-derived\n38\tconversational and language-use behaviors can support downstream language proficiency assessment. Results show that language-adapted Whisper models substantially improve language\n39\tdiarization performance for lower-resource and linguistically related Indian languages. Statistical\n40\tanalyses reveal that respondent speech ratio and intended language usage are strong predictors\n41\tof proficiency ratings. Furthermore, simple diarization-derived behavioral features achieve\n42\tperformance comparable to Whisper-based speech embeddings for proficiency prediction, while\n43\tcombining both yields the best results. Importantly, both the speech and language use statistical\n44\tanalyses and language proficiency prediction performance remain largely preserved when\n45\tusing fully automatic diarization outputs, demonstrating the potential of respondent-centric\n46\tconversational analysis for scalable language proficiency assessment.\n47\t\n48\t1. Introduction\n49\tSpoken language proficiency is a key indicator of communication competence and cognitive functioning, particularly among older adults (Roark et al., 2011). As populations continue to age worldwide, there is a growing\n50\tinterest in developing automatic methods for analyzing speech and language as scalable tools for clinical assessment,\n51\tcognitive monitoring, and large-scale epidemiological studies. In multilingual settings, spoken language proficiency\n52\tassessments are commonly used to evaluate an individual’s ability to communicate effectively in one or more languages\n53\tthrough measures of fluency, grammatical competence, and language use (Garcia and Gollan, 2026). However, most\n54\texisting automatic cognitive and language proficiency assessment systems focus primarily on lexical, acoustic, or\n55\tpronunciation-related features extracted from single-speaker recordings, while relatively little attention has been given\n56\tto multi-speaker and multilingual scenarios.\n57\tRecent advances in speech foundation models (Yang et al., 2024) have substantially improved performance\n58\tacross a wide range of speech processing tasks, including automatic speech recognition (ASR) (Prabhavalkar et al.,\n59\t2023), speaker diarization (Park et al., 2022), and language identification (O’Shaughnessy, 2025). These models\n60\tprovide rich contextualized speech representations that capture both acoustic and linguistic information. Nevertheless,\n61\tlanguage proficiency assessment in spontaneous conversational speech remains challenging. In interviewer–respondent\n62\tinteractions, the recorded audio contains speech from both participants, as well as pauses, nonverbal vocalizations, and\n63\toccasional overlapping speech. As a result, obtaining accurate respondent speech regions is a critical prerequisite for\n64\tdownstream language analysis. Furthermore, speech behaviors such as the respondent speech ratio, mean length of\n65\t⋆\n66\t\n67\tThis document is the result of the research project funded by National Institutes of Health (NIH) under Grant Nos. R01 AG051125, U01\n68\tAG064948, RF1 AG055273, and R01AG080473.\n69\t∗ Corresponding author\n70\tanfengxu@usc.edu (A. Xu)\n71\thttps://anfengxu136.github.io/ (A. Xu)\n72\tORCID (s): 0009-0001-5780-9797 (A. Xu)\n73\t\n74\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n75\t\n76\tPage 1 of 16\n77\t\n78\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n79\t\n80\tutterance (MLU), intended language use, and nonverbal vocalizations may provide valuable indicators of language\n81\tproficiency that are not explicitly captured by speech embeddings alone. Furthermore, in multilingual interview settings, particularly given the multiple linguistic backgrounds commonly found among individuals in India, respondents\n82\toften possess varying degrees of proficiency across multiple languages and may engage in code-mixing or codeswitching during spontaneous speech. As a result, language-use behaviors such as intended-language use may serve as\n83\tinformative indicators of proficiency, motivating the need for accurate language diarization in addition to respondent\n84\tspeech extraction.\n85\tTo enable such analyses, automatic systems must first identify the portions of the conversation corresponding\n86\tto the respondent and subsequently characterize language usage within those speech regions. While speaker role\n87\tdiarization (Kim et al., 2025; Xu et al., 2026a) and language diarization (Kalluri et al., 2024; Mishra et al., 2023;\n88\tMishra and Prasanna, 2024; Chua et al., 2023) have been studied as standalone speech processing tasks, their utility\n89\tfor downstream language proficiency assessments remains largely unexplored. In particular, it is unclear whether\n90\tautomatically extracted respondent speech regions and language usage patterns can provide reliable behavioral\n91\tmeasurements for language proficiency analysis and prediction.\n92\tTo address these gaps, we present a framework for respondent speech extraction, language diarization, and\n93\tlanguage proficiency prediction, using multilingual interview recordings collected as part of the Longitudinal Aging\n94\tStudy in India — Diagnostic Assessment of Dementia (LASI-DAD) (Lee et al., 2020). The dataset consists of\n95\tinterviewer–respondent interactions collected during a spoken language proficiency assessment involving spontaneous\n96\tstorytelling tasks of multiple languages in India. We manually annotate speaker-role and language labels by annotators\n97\twho are fluent in the corresponding languages used in individual interviews, enabling the study of respondent\n98\tparticipation patterns and multilingual language usage behaviors. Building upon speech foundation models, such as\n99\tWhisper (Radford et al., 2023), we develop a respondent speech extraction system implemented through speaker-role\n100\tdiarization, followed by a language diarization system that characterizes language usage within the extracted speech\n101\tregions.\n102\tBeyond diarization, we have rated the language proficiency scores for each recording by expert raters proficient\n103\tin the corresponding languages. We investigate how automatically derived conversational and language-use features\n104\trelate to language proficiency. Using bootstrap-based statistical analyses, we examine the associations between\n105\trespondent participation, utterance-level conversational patterns, language usage behaviors, and proficiency scores.\n106\tWe further evaluate language proficiency prediction using diarization-derived conversational features, acousticprosodic features, and speech foundation model embeddings. Through these analyses, we assess whether automatically\n107\tinferred respondent speech regions and language labels preserve meaningful information for downstream proficiency\n108\tassessment in older adults.\n109\tOur results demonstrate that conversational participation and language-use behaviors provide strong and interpretable indicators of language proficiency in older adults in India. In particular, respondent speech participation\n110\tand intended-language usage exhibit consistent positive associations with proficiency ratings, and these relationships\n111\tremain robust even when using fully automatic diarization outputs. Furthermore, simple diarization-derived conversational features achieve performance comparable to Whisper-based speech embeddings for language proficiency\n112\tprediction in Hindi, while combining the two yields the strongest regression and classification results. These findings\n113\thighlight the importance of respondent-centric conversational analysis and demonstrate the value of integrating\n114\tspeech foundation models with automatic respondent speech extraction and language diarization for scalable language\n115\tproficiency assessment in multilingual older-adult populations.\n116\tThe primary contributions of this work are as follows:\n117\t• We curate a multilingual older-adult interview-based speech dataset from India, with manually annotated\n118\tspeaker-role and language labels as well as spoken language proficiency ratings.\n119\t• We develop and evaluate Whisper-based speaker-role diarization and language diarization systems for automatic\n120\trespondent speech extraction and Indic language characterization.\n121\t• We conduct statistical analyses of conversational and language-use behaviors and show that respondent\n122\tspeech ratio and intended-language usage are significant predictors of language proficiency scores, providing\n123\tinterpretable behavioral markers compared to relying on speech representations from deep learning models.\n124\t\n125\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n126\t\n127\tPage 2 of 16\n128\t\n129\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n130\t\n131\t• We evaluate language proficiency prediction in Hindi using diarization-derived features, acoustic-prosodic\n132\tfeatures, and Whisper encoder representations. We show that simple diarization-derived features provide strong,\n133\tinterpretable, and complementary information comparable to Whisper-based embeddings.\n134\t• We demonstrate that both speech and language use statistical analyses and language proficiency prediction results\n135\tremain largely consistent when using automatically inferred speaker-role and language labels rather than manual\n136\tannotations, indicating that the proposed diarization systems preserve the key conversational and language-use\n137\tinformation required for reliable downstream assessment.\n138\t\n139\t2. Related Works\n140\t2.1. Speech Processing for Automatic Spoken Language Proficiency Assessment\n141\tEarly speech-based automatic language proficiency assessment approaches primarily relied on automatic speech\n142\trecognition (ASR) systems and handcrafted acoustic, prosodic, and linguistic features derived from speech and\n143\ttranscription outputs. For example, works such as (Wang et al., 2018; Gretter et al., 2019) developed an automatic\n144\tproficiency assessment system using ASR-generated transcripts and neural network models, demonstrating the\n145\tfeasibility of predicting spoken language proficiency directly from spontaneous speech responses. Similarly, (Knill\n146\tet al., 2018) studied the impact of ASR errors on automated spoken language assessment in question-answering tasks,\n147\twhere proficiency evaluation relied on linguistic features extracted from ASR-generated transcriptions. More recently,\n148\t(Kang et al., 2024) developed large-scale AI-based language tutoring systems that leverage end-to-end ASR for nonnative speech recognition and automatic fluency evaluation.\n149\tIn parallel, recent work has increasingly adopted self-supervised speech representations to improve the performance\n150\tover handcrafted features and reduce reliance on ASR-derived measurements, which are not always reliable under\n151\tlow-resource languages and populations. Works such as (Bannò and Matassoni, 2023; Bannò et al., 2023; Liu et al.,\n152\t2023) explored the use of a self-supervised learning (SSL) based speech foundation model, wav2vec 2.0 (Baevski\n153\tet al., 2020), for spoken language proficiency assessment and demonstrated that pretrained speech encoders capture\n154\tproficiency-related information directly from acoustic signals. More recently, (Ma et al., 2025) has explored Speech\n155\tLarge Language Models for L2 oral proficiency assessment, leveraging large-scale pretrained speech representations\n156\tto directly model proficiency from spoken responses.\n157\tDespite these advances, most prior studies focus on isolated speech recordings from language learners and assume\n158\tdirect access to the target speaker’s speech. In contrast, our work addresses multilingual interviewer–respondent\n159\tinteractions involving older adults, where the target respondent speech must first be extracted from conversational\n160\trecordings before downstream language analysis can be performed. We further investigate how automatically derived\n161\tconversational participation and language-use behaviors obtained through speaker-role and language diarization\n162\tcontribute to language proficiency assessment.\n163\t\n164\t2.2. Whisper as a Speech Foundation Model\n165\tRecent work has demonstrated that Whisper can serve as a powerful speech foundation model whose learned\n166\trepresentations encode diverse acoustic, linguistic, speaker, and behavioral characteristics beyond automatic speech\n167\trecognition. In the large-scale Vox-profile benchmark, (Feng et al., 2025) systematically evaluated speech foundation\n168\tmodels across a broad range of speaker and speech trait prediction tasks, including demographic attributes, vocal\n169\tcharacteristics, and speech behaviors. Their results showed that Whisper representations consistently capture rich\n170\tspeaker- and speech-related information and achieve competitive or superior performance compared to other speech\n171\tfoundation models, highlighting Whisper’s effectiveness as a general-purpose speech representation model.\n172\tWhisper has also shown strong performance in speaker diarization and speaker role diarization tasks. In child–adult\n173\tconversational speech, Xu et al. (2024) demonstrated that Whisper-based representations substantially outperform\n174\ttraditional speaker diarization approaches such as Pyannote (Plaquet and Bredin, 2023) and VBx (Pálka et al.,\n175\t2026), while also outperforming self-supervised learning models such as Wav2Vec 2.0 (Baevski et al., 2020) and\n176\tWavLM (Chen et al., 2022) for distinguishing child and adult speakers. Similar findings were reported by (Xu\n177\tet al., 2026b), which found that Whisper shows competitive diarization performance on older adults compared to\n178\tDiariZen (Han et al., 2025), a more recent diarization system that leverages WavLM.\n179\tBeyond speaker characterization, Whisper representations have also demonstrated strong capabilities for language\n180\tmodeling and clinical speech analysis. The VoxLect benchmark (Feng et al., 2026a) introduced a large-scale evaluation\n181\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n182\t\n183\tPage 3 of 16\n184\t\n185\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n186\tTable 1\n187\tDataset statistics. Duration statistics are reported at the recording level.\n188\tStatistic\n189\tNumber of Recordings\n190\tNumber of Interviewer Speakers\n191\tNumber of Respondent Speakers\n192\tRespondent Speaker Age Group† (years)\n193\tRespondent Speaker Gender†\n194\tRecording Duration (seconds)\n195\tAudio Duration\n196\tFull Recordings\n197\tRespondent Speech\n198\tInterviewer Speech\n199\t\n200\tValue\n201\t548\n202\t63\n203\t360\n204\tMean±Std: 71.2±6.7, Range: 60-105, Median: 70\n205\t143 Men, 124 Women, 93 Not Available\n206\tMean±Std: 63.8±31.2, Range: 20.8-344.7, Median: 65.1\n207\t9.71 hours\n208\t4.46 hours\n209\t0.94 hours\n210\t\n211\t† Statistics are calculated based on 267 respondents with demographic information available.\n212\t\n213\tframework for dialect and regional language identification across diverse languages worldwide and showed that\n214\tlanguage-adapted Whisper models consistently outperform SSL-based speech foundation models such as WavLM,\n215\thighlighting the language-discriminative properties of Whisper embeddings. Similar trends have been observed in\n216\tclinical speech analysis. In VoxCog (Feng et al., 2026b), which investigated multilingual cognitive impairment classification across diverse dialectal and linguistic conditions in older adults, Whisper-based models again outperformed\n217\tSSL-based architectures, including WavLM. These findings suggest that Whisper representations capture not only\n218\tlanguage and dialect information but also clinically relevant speech and linguistic characteristics associated with\n219\tcognitive decline, making them particularly suitable for downstream speech analysis tasks involving linguistically\n220\tand demographically diverse populations.\n221\tCollectively, these studies demonstrate that Whisper functions as a versatile speech foundation model capable of\n222\tmodeling speaker traits, language identity, and cognitive characteristics. Motivated by these findings, we adopt Whisper\n223\tas the backbone architecture for all stages of our framework, including speaker role diarization for respondent speech\n224\textraction, language diarization for multilingual language usage analysis, and Whisper-based language proficiency\n225\tprediction.\n226\t\n227\t3. Dataset Curation\n228\t3.1. Data Collection and Statistics\n229\tThe dataset is derived from the Wave-2 Objective Language Proficiency assessment within the Diagnostic\n230\tAssessment of Dementia component of the Longitudinal Aging Study in India (LASI-DAD) (Lee and Dey, 2020).\n231\tThe task is designed to evaluate multilingual spoken language proficiency among older adults in India through\n232\tspontaneous storytelling recordings. Multilingual participants are asked to narrate a familiar story in up to three\n233\treported languages, including stories such as Ramayana, Mahabharata, Akbar & Birbal, and other culturally familiar\n234\tnarratives. Alternatively, participants may describe a memorable festival or movie. The recordings are collected under\n235\trealistic conversational and environmental conditions and are subsequently rated by language proficiency raters for\n236\tfluency and grammatical competence on a five-point scale.\n237\tThe dataset contains multilingual conversational recordings involving an interviewer and an older adult respondent.\n238\tA single participant may contribute multiple recordings if they reported proficiency in multiple languages. The\n239\trecordings exhibit substantial variability in spontaneous speech patterns, conversational participation, language\n240\tmixing, recording quality, and speaking proficiency.\n241\tTable 1 summarizes the dataset statistics. In total, the dataset contains 548 recordings from 360 speakers,\n242\tcorresponding to approximately 9.71 hours of audio. The respondents’ speech accounts for approximately 4.46 hours,\n243\twhile the interviewers’ speech accounts for approximately 0.94 hours. The participants are older adults aged 60 to 105\n244\tyears (71.2 ± 6.7 years), with a relatively balanced gender distribution. The average recording duration is 63.8 seconds\n245\twith substantial variability due to differences in conversational fluency and response length.\n246\t\n247\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n248\t\n249\tPage 4 of 16\n250\t\n251\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n252\tTable 2\n253\tProficiency rating scale descriptors.\n254\tScore\n255\t1\n256\t\n257\t2\n258\t3\n259\t\n260\t4\n261\t5\n262\t\n263\tDescription\n264\tNon-fluent with grossly incomplete sentences. Speech is slow, hesitant, and strained except for\n265\tshort phrases; difficulty in perceiving continuity of speech; frequent grammatical errors even in simple\n266\tstructures; meaning is obscured.\n267\tPartially fluent with short sentences. Speech is frequently hesitant, with some sentences left\n268\tuncompleted; frequent grammatical errors even in simple structures that at times obscure meaning.\n269\tVague and repetitive fluent speech. Speech is relatively smooth; some hesitation and unevenness caused\n270\tby rephrasing and searching for words; may have many repetitions; frequent grammatical errors that do\n271\tnot obscure meaning; little variety in grammatical structures.\n272\tMostly complete and relevant sentences. Smooth and fluid speech; few hesitations; a slight search for\n273\twords; some errors in grammatical structures.\n274\tSentences of adequate length and complexity. Smooth and fluent speech; few to no hesitations; no\n275\tattempts to search for words; accuracy and variety of grammatical structures.\n276\t\n277\tTable 3\n278\tProficiency rating statistics and inter-rater reliability.\n279\tStatistic\n280\t\n281\tHindi\n282\t\n283\tTelugu\n284\t\n285\tMarathi\n286\t\n287\tEnglish\n288\t\n289\tGujarati\n290\t\n291\tOthers\n292\t\n293\tAll\n294\t\n295\tCount\n296\t\n297\t158\n298\t\n299\t99\n300\t\n301\t60\n302\t\n303\t57\n304\t\n305\t42\n306\t\n307\t122\n308\t\n309\t538\n310\t\n311\tProficiency Rating (mean±std)\n312\t\n313\t2.6±1.1\n314\t\n315\t2.4±0.6\n316\t\n317\t2.5±0.8\n318\t\n319\t2.9±1.2\n320\t\n321\t2.9±0.8\n322\t\n323\t2.8±1.0\n324\t\n325\t2.6±1.0\n326\t\n327\tInter-rater Reliability\n328\t- Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC)\n329\t- Quadratic-Weighted Kappa (𝜅𝑤 )\n330\t- Within-1 agreement\n331\t\n332\t0.559\n333\t0.391\n334\t70%\n335\t\n336\t0.079\n337\t0.049\n338\t72%\n339\t\n340\t0.400\n341\t0.159\n342\t64%\n343\t\n344\t0.513\n345\t0.417\n346\t78%\n347\t\n348\tN/A\n349\tN/A\n350\tN/A\n351\t\n352\tN/A\n353\tN/A\n354\tN/A\n355\t\n356\t0.454\n357\t0.303\n358\t71%\n359\t\n360\t3.2. Annotations\n361\t3.2.1. Language Proficiency Rating\n362\tThe recordings are evaluated by human annotators from an external transcription agency who are proficient in\n363\tthe corresponding languages. Each rater accesses the recordings through a dedicated rating platform and rates only\n364\tlanguages within their expertise. The primary proficiency score is based on a five-point scale assessing fluency and\n365\tgrammatical competence. The rating guidelines are listed in Table 2, which is a modified and abbreviated version of\n366\t(Garcia and Gollan, 2026). In addition to the proficiency rating, raters also annotate recording quality, the presence of\n367\tcode-mixing and code-switching, and whether the respondent spoke in the intended target language.\n368\tRaters may skip recordings when reliable evaluation is not possible due to factors such as poor audio quality or\n369\tthe respondent primarily speaking a language other than the prompted language. In this work, we use the fluency\n370\tand grammatical competence rating as the primary language proficiency score for downstream statistical analysis and\n371\tautomatic prediction experiments.\n372\tTable 3 summarizes proficiency-score statistics for the overall dataset and the five most frequently represented\n373\tlanguages. To assess rating reliability, co-authors familiar with Hindi, Telugu, Marathi, and English independently\n374\thave rated 104 recordings (at least 25 per language) using the same five-point scale. We compare these ratings with the\n375\toriginal ratings using Pearson correlation coefficient (PCC), quadratic-weighted kappa (𝜅𝑤 ), and within-1 agreement.\n376\tOverall, the ratings show moderate correspondence (PCC= 0.454, 𝜅𝑤 = 0.303, and 71% within-1 agreement). Interrater agreement is strongest for Hindi and English, more modest for Marathi, and negligible for Telugu. Given the low\n377\tinter-rater agreement for Telugu, we exclude Telugu from proficiency-based statistical and prediction analyses to avoid\n378\tdrawing conclusions from unreliable labels.\n379\t\n380\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n381\t\n382\tPage 5 of 16\n383\t\n384\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n385\tTable 4\n386\tLabel categories for interviewer and respondent speech segments.\n387\tLabel Category\n388\t\n389\tDescription\n390\t\n391\tLanguage ID\n392\t\n393\tIntelligible vocalizations containing words, annotated using ISO 639-3 language identifiers.\n394\t\n395\tUnintelligible Speech\n396\t\n397\tSpeech containing words that are not intelligible.\n398\t\n399\tNonverbal Vocalizations\n400\t\n401\tVocalizations without lexical content, such as laughter, “uh-huh”, or filled pauses.\n402\t\n403\tFigure 1: Illustration of speaker role diarization and language diarization pipelines.\n404\t\n405\t3.2.2. Speaker Role and Language Diarization Annotation\n406\tWe manually annotate both speaker role diarization and language diarization labels using Praat software. Each\n407\trecording is annotated with three tiers: interviewer, respondent, and background-speech. Annotators create utterancelevel speech boundaries for conversational speech segments and assign corresponding speaker-role and language labels.\n408\tTo improve annotation consistency, we follow several temporal annotation constraints. Speech segments shorter than\n409\t0.1 s are not annotated, while pauses shorter than 0.2 s between speech from the same speaker are not segmented into\n410\tseparate utterances. Annotators are instructed to place speech boundaries with less than approximately 0.1–0.2 s errors.\n411\tFor the interviewer and respondent tiers, each speech segment is assigned one of three label categories as detailed in\n412\tTable 4.\n413\t\n414\t4. Method\n415\tFig. 1 presents the multi-stage computational pipelines, including speaker role diarization and language diarization\n416\tmodelings, followed by the downstream modeling. At the beginning, the dyadic conversational audio is processed\n417\tby a speaker role diarization model to identify the speech segments associated with the respondent. Subsequently,\n418\tlanguage diarization is applied to the identified respondent speech segments to classify the spoken languages within\n419\teach segment. We perform speaker role diarization before language diarization because we found it to be a more robust\n420\ttask, thereby minimizing error propagation in the downstream language diarization stage.\n421\t\n422\t4.1. Speaker Role Diarization Modeling for Respondent Speech Extraction\n423\tWe conduct respondent speech extraction via speaker role diarization modeling formulated as a frame-level\n424\tclassification problem. Specifically, the task is to classify each frame as interviewer, respondent, silence, or overlapping\n425\tspeech. Given an audio feature input 𝑋 = [𝑥1 , ..., 𝑥𝑇 ], the goal is to predict the speaker-role label 𝑦𝑡 for 𝑥𝑡 at each\n426\ttimestamp 𝑡. Since this work is primarily developed based on the Whisper-family models, the input 𝑋 is defined as a\n427\t30-second audio feature segment with 𝑇 = 1500 frames. This corresponds to the maximum frame length supported\n428\tby the Whisper encoder at a frame shift of 20 ms. Here, 𝑦𝑡 ∈ {𝑖𝑛𝑡𝑒𝑟, 𝑟𝑒𝑠, 𝑠𝑖𝑙, 𝑜𝑙𝑝}, where 𝑖𝑛𝑡𝑒𝑟, 𝑟𝑒𝑠, 𝑠𝑖𝑙, and 𝑜𝑙𝑝 denote\n429\tinterviewer, respondent, silence, and overlapping speech, respectively.\n430\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n431\t\n432\tPage 6 of 16\n433\t\n434\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n435\t\n436\tFigure 2: Illustration of speaker role and language diarization pipeline.\n437\t\n438\tThe overall modeling pipeline is illustrated in Fig. 1. We keep the Whisper encoder frozen and employ LowRank Adaptation (LoRA) on the feed-forward layers. The input waveform is first processed by the Whisper encoder to\n439\tobtain hidden representations, followed by a learnable weighted average across encoder layers. The resulting features\n440\tare passed through a stack of three 1D CNN layers, each with 256 channels, ReLU activation, and dropout with a\n441\tprobability of 0.1. A final 1D CNN layer with kernel size 1 produces frame-level predictions.\n442\t\n443\t4.2. Language Diarization Modeling\n444\tFollowing the respondent speech extraction from speaker role diarization, we perform language diarization on the\n445\tdetected speech regions. Since language labels are only assigned to speech frames, silence regions are excluded from\n446\tthis stage. Here, the target labels are 𝑦𝑡 ∈ {𝐻𝑖𝑛, 𝐸𝑛𝑔, 𝑇 𝑒𝑙, 𝑀𝑎𝑟, 𝐺𝑢𝑗, 𝑂𝑡ℎ𝑒𝑟, 𝑉 𝑜}, where 𝐻𝑖𝑛, 𝐸𝑛𝑔, 𝑇 𝑒𝑙, 𝑀𝑎𝑟, and\n447\t𝐺𝑢𝑗 denote Hindi, English, Telugu, Marathi, and Gujarati, respectively. The label 𝑂𝑡ℎ𝑒𝑟 represents languages outside\n448\tthese target languages, while 𝑉 𝑜 denotes nonverbal vocalizations.\n449\tWe use the same Whisper-based architecture as described in Section 4.1. The only difference is the output space:\n450\tthe final prediction layer has four output channels for speaker role diarization and seven output channels for language\n451\tdiarization. During both training and inference, the entire audio segment is processed by the model. During training,\n452\tthe ground-truth annotation is used, and supervision is applied to both interviewer and respondent speech regions\n453\twhile silence, unintelligible speech, and overlapping speech regions are masked out for back-propagation. During\n454\tinference, language predictions are generated for all frames, but only speech regions corresponding to the interviewer\n455\tand respondent are retained, either from manual annotations or from the inferred speaker-role diarization outputs. For\n456\tdownstream statistical analysis and language proficiency prediction, only the respondent speech regions are used. For\n457\tlanguage diarization evaluation, overlapping speech and unintelligible speech regions are excluded from scoring.\n458\t\n459\t4.3. Diarization Features and Statistical Analysis\n460\tTo analyze the relationship between diarization features and proficiency ratings, we perform bootstrap-based\n461\tOrdinary Least Squares (OLS) regression analyses using both speaker role diarization and language diarization features\n462\textracted from the respondent speech segments. The dependent variable is the proficiency rating score, while the\n463\tindependent variables consist of normalized conversational features and language identity indicators. The extracted\n464\tspeaker role and language diarization features are listed in Table 5.\n465\tFor both speaker and language diarization analyses, we compare features extracted from ground-truth annotations\n466\tand features derived from automatically inferred diarization outputs. This comparison evaluates the robustness of the\n467\tstatistical relationships under realistic automatic diarization conditions. The OLS model is formulated as:\n468\t𝑦=\n469\t\n470\t𝑀\n471\t∑\n472\t𝑖=1\n473\t\n474\t𝛽𝑖 𝑥 𝑖 +\n475\t\n476\t𝐾\n477\t∑\n478\t\n479\t𝛾𝑘 𝟙{lang = 𝑘} + 𝜖,\n480\t\n481\t𝑘=1\n482\t\n483\twhere 𝑦 denotes the proficiency rating, 𝑥𝑖 represents the normalized diarization-derived features, 𝛽𝑖 and 𝛾𝑘 are\n484\tregression coefficients, 𝜖 is the residual error term, and 𝑀 and 𝐾 are the number of diarization features and the number\n485\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n486\t\n487\tPage 7 of 16\n488\t\n489\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n490\tTable 5\n491\tExtracted features for speaker diarization and language diarization analysis.\n492\tAnalysis\n493\t\n494\tSpeaker Role Diarization\n495\t\n496\tLanguage Diarization\n497\t\n498\tFeature\n499\t\n500\tDescription\n501\t\n502\tSpeech Ratio\n503\t\n504\tTotal respondent speech duration divided by total\n505\taudio duration.\n506\tMean duration of respondent utterances.\n507\t\n508\tUtterance Length (mean)\n509\tUtterance Length (std)\n510\t\n511\tStandard deviation of respondent utterance durations.\n512\t\n513\tIntended Language Ratio\n514\t\n515\tProportion of intended-language speech within respondent utterances.\n516\tProportion of nonverbal vocalizations within respondent utterances.\n517\t\n518\tVocalization Ratio\n519\t\n520\tof languages, respectively. Additionally, 𝟙{lang = 𝑘} is the language indicator function that models language-specific\n521\toffsets across language conditions. All continuous features are normalized prior to regression. For this experiment,\n522\twe use the English, Hindi, and Marathi subsets, as these languages contain sufficient numbers of recordings, adequate\n523\tvariability in proficiency rating scores, and modest inter-rater agreement scores for reliable statistical analysis.\n524\tTo assess coefficient stability and statistical significance, we conduct a bootstrap experiment with 10000 iterations.\n525\tIn each iteration, we randomly sample 80% of the recordings from each language subset and fit the OLS model\n526\tindependently. We then compute the mean and standard deviation of each regression coefficient across bootstrap runs,\n527\talong with the proportion of runs in which the corresponding 𝑝-value is below 0.05. We additionally report the mean\n528\tadjusted 𝑅2 across all bootstrap iterations.\n529\t\n530\t4.4. Language Proficiency Prediction\n531\tWe further investigate automatic prediction of language proficiency scores using three feature categories:\n532\t• Diarization-derived Features: includes respondent speech ratio, utterance length (std), intended language\n533\tusage, and vocalization ratio, as described in Section 4.3. We exclude utterance length (mean), as it has shown\n534\tno significance for language proficiency scores, as shown in Section 6.3.\n535\t• OpenSMILE Features: handcrafted acoustic-prosodic features extracted using the OpenSMILE (Eyben et al.,\n536\t2010). We use the eGeMAPSv02 (Eyben et al., 2015) feature set consisting of 88 descriptors, capturing acoustic\n537\tand prosodic characteristics such as pitch, energy, MFCC, jitter, shimmer, and harmonics-to-noise ratio.\n538\t• Whisper Encoder Features: speech representations obtained from a Whisper-small encoder from VoxLect,\n539\tproviding contextualized speech embeddings learned from large-scale speech pretraining.\n540\tSince Hindi contains the largest number of recordings, sufficient score variability, and relatively strong inter-rater\n541\tagreement score, we conduct the prediction experiments on the Hindi subset. We evaluate both a regression task that\n542\tdirectly predicts the continuous proficiency score on the 1–5 scale and a binary classification task separating low\n543\tproficiency (scores 1–2) from higher proficiency (scores 3–5).\n544\tFor the diarization-derived and OpenSMILE feature sets, we apply ridge regression for score prediction and\n545\tlogistic regression for binary classification. Both feature sets are computed using either oracle or automatically inferred\n546\trespondent speaker regions. For inferred speaker regions, adjacent utterances separated by less than 0.2 s are merged,\n547\twhile utterances shorter than 0.2 s are discarded to reduce segmentation noise. The diarization-derived features are\n548\tthen computed from the resulting respondent speech regions. For the OpenSMILE-based approach, the processed\n549\trespondent speech segments are concatenated prior to feature extraction. All handcrafted features are normalized prior\n550\tto training.\n551\tFor the Whisper-based approach, recordings are processed using sliding windows of up to 30 s with a hop size of\n552\t15 s during both training and inference. The waveform is passed through a Whisper-small encoder to obtain hidden\n553\trepresentations, while the training objective is applied only to respondent speech regions and all other regions are\n554\tmasked out. We evaluate both oracle respondent regions and automatically inferred respondent regions obtained from\n555\tthe speaker diarization system. Audio segments shorter than 10 s at the end of a recording are discarded. For binary\n556\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n557\t\n558\tPage 8 of 16\n559\t\n560\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n561\t\n562\tclassification, the model is trained using cross-entropy loss, while regression uses mean squared error (MSE) loss to\n563\tdirectly estimate the 1–5 proficiency score.\n564\tWe additionally evaluate a simple late-fusion ensemble strategy. For regression, the final predicted score is\n565\tobtained by averaging the outputs from the individual models. For binary classification, we average the predicted\n566\tclass probabilities across models and assign the final label based on the resulting aggregated probability distribution.\n567\t\n568\t5. Experimental Settings\n569\t5.1. Metric for Speaker Role & Language Diarization\n570\tWe evaluate both speaker role diarization and language diarization using the diarization error rate (DER). DER is\n571\tcomputed with the following formula:\n572\tDER =\n573\t\n574\tMD + FA + Conf .\n575\t,\n576\tTotal\n577\t\n578\t(1)\n579\t\n580\twhere MD denotes missed speech, FA denotes false alarm speech, Conf . denotes label confusion, and Total. denotes\n581\ttotal reference speech duration. We use a collar tolerance of 0s around the utterance boundaries, and we include\n582\toverlapping speech regions only for speaker role diarization evaluation, as those regions are masked out for language\n583\tdiarization. For speaker role diarization, the confusion term corresponds to speaker role confusion (SC), i.e., speech\n584\tassigned to the wrong speaker role, such as interviewer versus respondent. Therefore, speaker role DER is:\n585\tDERspk =\n586\t\n587\tMD + FA + SC\n588\t.\n589\tTotal\n590\t\n591\t(2)\n592\t\n593\tFor language diarization, the confusion term corresponds to language confusion (LC), i.e., speech assigned to the\n594\twrong language or vocalization category. Therefore, language DER is computed as:\n595\tDERlang =\n596\t\n597\tMD + FA + LC\n598\t.\n599\tTotal\n600\t\n601\t(3)\n602\t\n603\tFor language diarization with oracle speech activity regions, MD and FA are zero by construction, and the DER is\n604\ttherefore equivalent to the language confusion rate.\n605\t\n606\t5.2. Data Splits and Cross-Validation\n607\tAll experiments for diarization and language proficiency prediction are conducted using the same five-fold crossvalidation split. The data are partitioned at the speaker level, ensuring that no respondent or interviewer appears in\n608\tmore than one fold. Furthermore, recordings from each language are distributed as evenly as possible across the five\n609\tfolds to maintain comparable language distributions throughout evaluation. For each cross-validation iteration, one fold\n610\tis used for testing, while the remaining four folds are used for model development. Within the development portion,\n611\t25% of the recordings are randomly selected as a validation set, with the remaining 75% used for training. The same\n612\tspeaker-disjoint constraint is enforced between the training and validation sets.\n613\t\n614\t5.3. Experimental Details for Speaker Role & Language Diarization\n615\tFor speaker role diarization and language diarization, models are trained independently on each cross-validation\n616\tsplit. Evaluation is performed at the recording level, and the final reported metrics are obtained by averaging the testset results across the five folds. This procedure ensures that each recording is evaluated exactly once while providing\n617\ta robust estimate of overall performance.\n618\tFor all diarization experiments, we train the models for 50 epochs using the Adam optimizer with a weight decay\n619\tof 1 × 10−4 . We use a learning rate of 2 × 10−3 for all diarization experiments, except for Whisper-VoxLect Large, for\n620\twhich we use a smaller learning rate of 5 × 10−4 , as higher learning rates result in unstable training. During training,\n621\tonly the LoRA modules inserted into the feed-forward layers of the Transformer encoder and the diarization head are\n622\tupdated, while all other model parameters remain frozen. We use a LoRA rank of 64 for the Whisper-VoxLect variants,\n623\tinitializing both the LoRA parameters and the 1D CNN layers of the diarization head, except for the final output layer,\n624\tfrom the corresponding VoxLect checkpoints. For the Whisper-Original variants, we use a smaller LoRA rank of 16,\n625\twhich we found to be more stable when training from randomly initialized LoRA parameters. We use a single NVIDIA\n626\tRTX A6000 48GB GPU for all the experiments.\n627\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n628\t\n629\tPage 9 of 16\n630\t\n631\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n632\tTable 6\n633\tSpeaker role diarization with different Whisper model families. The numbers are reported as percentages (%)\n634\tWhisper-Small\n635\t\n636\tWhisper-Large\n637\t\n638\tInitialization\n639\t\n640\tDER\n641\t\n642\tMD\n643\t\n644\tFA\n645\t\n646\tSC\n647\t\n648\tDER\n649\t\n650\tMD\n651\t\n652\tFA\n653\t\n654\tSC\n655\t\n656\tWhisper-Original\n657\tWhisper-VoxLect\n658\t\n659\t21.62\n660\t20.76\n661\t\n662\t9.11\n663\t8.50\n664\t\n665\t6.74\n666\t7.26\n667\t\n668\t5.77\n669\t5.00\n670\t\n671\t20.43\n672\t21.01\n673\t\n674\t9.42\n675\t8.35\n676\t\n677\t6.71\n678\t7.68\n679\t\n680\t4.30\n681\t4.98\n682\t\n683\tTable 7\n684\tLanguage diarization results with different Whisper model families. The numbers are reported as percentages (%).\n685\tWhisper-Small\n686\tInitialization\n687\tWhisper-Original\n688\tWhisper-VoxLect\n689\t\n690\tWhisper-Large\n691\t\n692\tVAD\n693\t\n694\tDER\n695\t\n696\tMD\n697\t\n698\tFA\n699\t\n700\tLC\n701\t\n702\tDER\n703\t\n704\tMD\n705\t\n706\tFA\n707\t\n708\tLC\n709\t\n710\tOracle\n711\tPredicted\n712\tOracle\n713\tPredicted\n714\t\n715\t47.71\n716\t60.82\n717\t28.31\n718\t40.38\n719\t\n720\t0\n721\t8.21\n722\t0\n723\t7.62\n724\t\n725\t0\n726\t7.06\n727\t0\n728\t7.56\n729\t\n730\t47.71\n731\t45.56\n732\t28.31\n733\t25.20\n734\t\n735\t35.03\n736\t46.17\n737\t27.97\n738\t40.47\n739\t\n740\t0\n741\t8.56\n742\t0\n743\t7.49\n744\t\n745\t0\n746\t7.03\n747\t0\n748\t8.05\n749\t\n750\t35.03\n751\t30.59\n752\t27.97\n753\t24.93\n754\t\n755\t5.4. Experimental Details for Language Proficiency Prediction\n756\tFor the language proficiency prediction experiments, we follow the same five-fold cross-validation protocol.\n757\tRather than averaging metrics independently across folds, we first aggregate the predictions from all test recordings\n758\tacross the five folds to form a single set of pooled out-of-sample predictions covering the entire dataset. The final\n759\tevaluation metrics are then computed on this pooled prediction set. For experiments using inferred speaker and\n760\tlanguage diarization outputs, we use exactly the same train, validation, and test splits as the corresponding oracle\n761\texperiments. The speaker role diarization and language diarization systems are first applied to all recordings within\n762\teach split, and the resulting inferred boundaries and labels are used for subsequent feature extraction and model training.\n763\tConsequently, both the training and evaluation stages operate entirely on automatically inferred diarization outputs,\n764\tallowing us to assess the impact of diarization errors under a realistic deployment scenario while maintaining a fair\n765\tcomparison with the oracle condition.\n766\tFor Whisper-based language proficiency prediction, we adopt the same architecture used for the diarization\n767\texperiments, except that we apply temporal average pooling after the second CNN layer of the diarization head. The\n768\tpooled representation is then passed to a linear output layer with a single output unit for both regression and binary\n769\tclassification. We use a learning rate of 2 × 10−3 , and all other training setups follow the same settings as for the\n770\tdiarization experiments.\n771\t\n772\t6. Results\n773\t6.1. Speaker Role & Language Diarization Results\n774\t6.1.1. Speaker Role Diarization Results\n775\tTable 6 presents the speaker role diarization results using Whisper-based encoder representations. We compare\n776\tWhisper-Original and Whisper-VoxLect initialization across Whisper-Small and Whisper-Large encoder configurations. Overall, all model configurations achieve very similar performance, with DER values ranging from 20.43%\n777\tto 21.62%. The error patterns are also largely comparable across systems, with similar missed detection and false\n778\talarm rates. Speaker confusion remains relatively low for all configurations, ranging from 4.30% to 5.77%, indicating\n779\tthat the models are generally effective at distinguishing interviewer and respondent speech. These results suggest that\n780\tboth Whisper-Original and Whisper-VoxLect representations provide reliable speaker-role information for respondent\n781\tspeech extraction, with performance remaining stable across encoder scales and initialization conditions.\n782\t\n783\t6.1.2. Language Diarization Results\n784\tTable 7 presents the language diarization results using Whisper-Small and Whisper-Large encoders under both\n785\tOracle and inferred VAD conditions. As expected, inferred VAD degrades performance relative to Oracle VAD for all\n786\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n787\t\n788\tPage 10 of 16\n789\t\n790\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n791\t\n792\tFigure 3: Confusion matrices for language diarization comparing Whisper-Original and Whisper-VoxLect using WhisperSmall. All numbers are reported in percentage (%).\n793\t\n794\tsystems due to additional missed detection (MD) and false alarm (FA) errors introduced by the speaker-role diarization\n795\tstage. Overall, Whisper-VoxLect substantially improves language diarization performance across both model sizes and\n796\tVAD conditions. Under Oracle VAD, the language confusion (LC) decreases from 47.71% to 28.31% for WhisperSmall and from 35.03% to 27.97% for Whisper-Large. Similar improvements are observed under inferred VAD, where\n797\tLC decreases from 45.56% to 25.20% for Whisper-Small and from 30.59% to 24.93% for Whisper-Large. These\n798\tconsistent reductions indicate that language-identification-oriented adaptation produces substantially more languagediscriminative representations for frame-level language attribution. Under the original Whisper initialization, WhisperLarge achieves lower LC than Whisper-Small, while after VoxLect adaptation, both model sizes achieve very similar\n799\tperformance. This suggests that the benefits of VoxLect adaptation are robust across encoder scales and are not limited\n800\tto a particular model size.\n801\tThe confusion matrices in Figure 3 provide additional insight into the performance gains from VoxLect adaptation.\n802\tThe largest improvements are observed for Telugu, Marathi, and Gujarati, which are among the most challenging\n803\tlanguage categories for the original Whisper model. For Telugu, recall increases from 28.1% to 63.5%, while confusion\n804\twith Hindi decreases from 26.9% to 2.3%. Similarly, Marathi recall improves from 7.8% to 50.3%, accompanied by\n805\tsubstantial reductions in confusion with Hindi (51.6% to 15.4%) and Other languages (23.0% to 12.9%). Gujarati\n806\texhibits a similar trend, with recall increasing from 4.0% to 45.0%, while confusion with Hindi decreases from 50.2%\n807\tto 24.8% and confusion with Other languages decreases from 32.2% to 11.1%.\n808\tIn contrast, languages that already achieve relatively strong performance with the original Whisper model, such\n809\tas Hindi and English, exhibit smaller gains. Hindi recall improves from 71.3% to 89.3%, while English performance\n810\tremains largely unchanged (87.3% versus 85.1%). The Other language category also benefits substantially, with recall\n811\tincreasing from 52.4% to 75.5%. Overall, these findings suggest that VoxLect adaptation is particularly effective at\n812\treducing confusion among closely related Indic languages, leading to substantially more discriminative language\n813\trepresentations for lower-resource language categories.\n814\t\n815\t6.2. Statistical Analysis Results\n816\t6.2.1. Speaker Diarization Features\n817\tTable 8 presents the bootstrap OLS analysis using speaker diarization features derived from both oracle annotations\n818\tand automatically inferred speaker-role diarization outputs. We report the mean regression coefficients and the\n819\tproportion of bootstrap runs in which each feature achieves statistical significance (𝑝 < 0.05).\n820\tAcross both oracle and inferred settings, speech ratio consistently emerges as the strongest predictor of language\n821\tproficiency ratings. The feature exhibits stable positive coefficients (0.391 ± 0.036 and 0.368 ± 0.037, respectively) and\n822\treaches statistical significance in 100% of bootstrap runs under both conditions. This finding suggests that respondents\n823\twho contribute a larger proportion of the interview conversation tend to receive higher proficiency ratings.\n824\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n825\t\n826\tPage 11 of 16\n827\t\n828\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n829\tTable 8\n830\tOLS analysis using speaker diarization features. Oracle and Inferred denote annotation-derived and automatically inferred\n831\tspeaker-role regions, respectively. Coefficients are reported as mean ± standard deviation across runs.\n832\tOracle\n833\tFeature\n834\tSpeech Ratio\n835\tUtterance Length (mean)\n836\tUtterance Length (std)\n837\t\n838\tInferred\n839\t\n840\tCoef.\n841\t\n842\t𝑃 (𝑝 < 0.05)\n843\t\n844\tCoef.\n845\t\n846\t𝑃 (𝑝 < 0.05)\n847\t\n848\t0.391±0.036\n849\t0.009±0.054\n850\t0.034±0.054\n851\t\n852\t100.00%\n853\t0.01%\n854\t0.03%\n855\t\n856\t0.368±0.037\n857\t-0.028±0.049\n858\t0.135±0.045\n859\t\n860\t100.00%\n861\t0.00%\n862\t4.55%\n863\t\n864\tAdjusted 𝑅2\n865\t\n866\t0.153±0.021\n867\t\n868\t0.167±0.022\n869\t\n870\tTable 9\n871\tOLS analysis using language diarization features. Oracle and Inferred denote annotation-derived and automatically inferred\n872\tlanguage features, respectively. Coefficients are reported as mean ± standard deviation across runs.\n873\tOracle\n874\tFeature\n875\tIntended Language Prob.\n876\tNonverbal Vocalization Prob.\n877\tAdjusted 𝑅2\n878\t\n879\tInferred (Oracle VAD)\n880\t\n881\tInferred (Predicted VAD)\n882\t\n883\tCoef.\n884\t\n885\t𝑃 (𝑝 < 0.05)\n886\t\n887\tCoef.\n888\t\n889\t𝑃 (𝑝 < 0.05)\n890\t\n891\tCoef.\n892\t\n893\t𝑃 (𝑝 < 0.05)\n894\t\n895\t0.341±0.031\n896\t-0.144±0.035\n897\t\n898\t100.00%\n899\t35.54%\n900\t\n901\t0.235±0.037\n902\t-0.266±0.034\n903\t\n904\t96.81%\n905\t99.93%\n906\t\n907\t0.246±0.039\n908\t-0.212±0.042\n909\t\n910\t97.42%\n911\t94.24%\n912\t\n913\t0.160±0.020\n914\t\n915\t0.149±0.022\n916\t\n917\t0.124±0.021\n918\t\n919\tIn contrast, mean utterance length shows coefficients close to zero and is almost never statistically significant,\n920\tindicating little association with language proficiency in this dataset. Utterance length variability exhibits a modest\n921\tpositive relationship with proficiency. Under oracle speaker regions, the coefficient remains small (0.034 ± 0.054) and\n922\tis significant in only 0.03% of bootstrap runs. When inferred speaker regions are used, the coefficient increases to\n923\t0.135 ± 0.045, although the feature still achieves statistical significance in only 4.55% of bootstrap runs. These results\n924\tindicate that utterance-level duration statistics contribute substantially less information than overall conversational\n925\tparticipation.\n926\tThe overall regression performance remains highly consistent between oracle and automatically inferred speaker\n927\tregions. The adjusted 𝑅2 is 0.153 ± 0.021 for oracle annotations and 0.167 ± 0.022 for inferred speaker regions,\n928\tindicating that the automatically derived respondent speech segments preserve the primary conversational patterns\n929\tassociated with language proficiency. Overall, these findings suggest that respondent participation is the dominant\n930\tconversational predictor of proficiency ratings, while the proposed speaker-role diarization system provides sufficiently\n931\treliable respondent speech extraction for downstream behavioral analyses.\n932\t\n933\t6.2.2. Language Diarization Features\n934\tTable 9 presents the bootstrap OLS analysis using language diarization features under three conditions: oracle\n935\tannotations, inferred language diarization with oracle VAD, and fully inferred language diarization with predicted\n936\tVAD obtained from speaker role diarization. The reported coefficients correspond to the mean and standard deviation\n937\tacross 10,000 bootstrap iterations.\n938\tAcross all experimental settings, the intended language ratio exhibits a consistent positive association with language\n939\tproficiency ratings. The feature achieves statistical significance in nearly all bootstrap runs, with significance rates of\n940\t100.0%, 96.8%, and 97.4% for the oracle, inferred language diarization with oracle VAD, and fully inferred settings,\n941\trespectively. The corresponding regression coefficients remain positive across conditions (0.341±0.031, 0.235±0.037,\n942\tand 0.246 ± 0.039), indicating that respondents who spend a larger proportion of their speech in the intended target\n943\tlanguage tend to receive higher proficiency ratings. Although the coefficients decrease when inferred language labels\n944\tare used, the overall relationship remains highly stable, suggesting that intended language usage is a robust behavioral\n945\tmarker of language proficiency even under automatic diarization conditions.\n946\tNonverbal vocalization ratio demonstrates a consistent negative relationship with proficiency ratings. Under oracle\n947\tannotations, the feature exhibits a modest negative coefficient (−0.144 ± 0.035) and reaches statistical significance\n948\tin 35.5% of bootstrap runs. Interestingly, the relationship becomes substantially stronger under automatic language\n949\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n950\t\n951\tPage 12 of 16\n952\t\n953\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n954\tTable 10\n955\tLinear regression results. Oracle and Inferred denote annotation-derived and automatically inferred speaker-role and\n956\tlanguage regions, respectively. PCC denotes Pearson Correlation Coefficient.\n957\tPCC\n958\t\n959\tMAE\n960\t\n961\tFeature\n962\t\n963\tOracle\n964\t\n965\tInferred\n966\t\n967\tOracle\n968\t\n969\tInferred\n970\t\n971\tDiarization-derived Features\n972\tOpenSMILE Features\n973\tWhisper Encoder Features\n974\t\n975\t0.441\n976\t0.177\n977\t0.528\n978\t\n979\t0.440\n980\t0.175\n981\t0.527\n982\t\n983\t0.794\n984\t0.996\n985\t0.732\n986\t\n987\t0.806\n988\t1.000\n989\t0.725\n990\t\n991\tEnsemble (Diarization + Whisper)\n992\t\n993\t0.531\n994\t\n995\t0.530\n996\t\n997\t0.744\n998\t\n999\t0.747\n1000\t\n1001\tTable 11\n1002\tClassification analysis. Oracle and Inferred denote annotation-derived and automatically inferred speaker-role and language\n1003\tregions, respectively. The results are shown in percentages.\n1004\tAccuracy (%)\n1005\t\n1006\tF1-Macro (%)\n1007\t\n1008\tFeature\n1009\t\n1010\tOracle\n1011\t\n1012\tInferred\n1013\t\n1014\tOracle\n1015\t\n1016\tInferred\n1017\t\n1018\tDiarization-derived Features\n1019\tOpenSMILE Features\n1020\tWhisper Encoder Features\n1021\t\n1022\t61.39\n1023\t53.80\n1024\t59.49\n1025\t\n1026\t62.03\n1027\t54.43\n1028\t59.49\n1029\t\n1030\t61.39\n1031\t53.75\n1032\t59.47\n1033\t\n1034\t62.00\n1035\t54.42\n1036\t59.47\n1037\t\n1038\tEnsemble (Diarization + Whisper)\n1039\t\n1040\t64.56\n1041\t\n1042\t65.82\n1043\t\n1044\t64.55\n1045\t\n1046\t65.80\n1047\t\n1048\tdiarization, with coefficients of −0.266 ± 0.034 and −0.212 ± 0.042 and significance rates of 99.9% and 94.2% for\n1049\tthe oracle-VAD and predicted-VAD settings, respectively. These results suggest that a higher proportion of nonverbal\n1050\tvocalizations is associated with lower language proficiency. One possible explanation for the stronger effect under\n1051\tinferred language diarization is that less proficient speakers tend to produce more disfluent speech. These regions may\n1052\treceive higher nonverbal vocalization probabilities from the language diarization model, whereas human annotators\n1053\tmay be more likely to label them as intended-language speech. As a result, the inferred vocalization ratio may capture\n1054\tbroader speech disfluency patterns beyond true nonverbal vocalizations, leading to a stronger association with language\n1055\tproficiency.\n1056\tThe overall explanatory power of the regression model decreases as diarization errors are introduced, with adjusted\n1057\t𝑅2 values declining from 0.160 ± 0.020 for oracle annotations to 0.149 ± 0.022 and 0.124 ± 0.021 for the inferred\n1058\tlanguage diarization conditions. Nevertheless, both intended language usage and nonverbal vocalization behaviors\n1059\tretain consistent directional relationships with proficiency ratings across all settings. These findings indicate that\n1060\tlanguage-use patterns extracted from automatic speaker role and language diarization preserve the key behavioral\n1061\tsignals required for downstream proficiency-related analyses.\n1062\t\n1063\t6.3. Language Proficiency Prediction\n1064\tTables 10 and 11 present the language proficiency prediction results for the regression and binary classification\n1065\ttasks, respectively, using diarization-derived conversational features, OpenSMILE acoustic-prosodic features, Whisper\n1066\tencoder representations. We also report ensemble results combining predictions from diarization-derived and Whisper\n1067\tencoder features.\n1068\tFor the regression task in Table 10, the Whisper encoder features achieve the strongest individual performance,\n1069\tobtaining PCC values of 0.528 and 0.527 under oracle and inferred speaker regions, respectively, while also\n1070\tachieving the lowest MAE of 0.732 and 0.725, respectively. The diarization-derived conversational features perform\n1071\tcompetitively, reaching PCC values of 0.441 and 0.440, despite relying on only a small set of high-level behavioral\n1072\tfeatures. In contrast, the OpenSMILE feature set performs substantially worse, achieving PCC values below 0.18\n1073\tand MAE values of around 1.00. The relatively strong performance of the diarization-derived features suggests that\n1074\tconversational participation patterns and language-use behaviors contain substantial information relevant to language\n1075\tproficiency assessment.\n1076\t\n1077\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n1078\t\n1079\tPage 13 of 16\n1080\t\n1081\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n1082\t\n1083\tCombining the diarization-derived and Whisper-based systems through simple prediction averaging yields the\n1084\tstrongest overall regression performance. The ensemble achieves PCC values of 53.12% and 53.01% under oracle and\n1085\tinferred speaker regions, respectively, while maintaining low MAE values of 0.74 and 0.75. Although the improvements\n1086\tover the Whisper-only system are modest, the consistent gains indicate that the diarization-derived behavioral features\n1087\tprovide complementary information beyond the acoustic and linguistic representations captured by Whisper encoder\n1088\tembeddings. In particular, the behavioral features encode higher-level conversational dynamics and language-use\n1089\tstatistics that are not explicitly represented in the speech embeddings.\n1090\tFor the binary classification task in Table 11, the overall trends differ somewhat from the regression results. The\n1091\tdiarization-derived features emerge as the strongest individual feature set, achieving accuracies of 61.39% and 62.03%\n1092\tand F1-Macro scores of 61.39% and 62.00% under oracle and inferred speaker regions, respectively. These results\n1093\toutperform both the Whisper encoder features and the OpenSMILE features, further highlighting the importance of\n1094\tconversational participation and language-use behaviors for distinguishing lower-proficiency speakers from higherproficiency speakers. The ensemble model again achieves the best overall performance, reaching accuracies of 64.56%\n1095\tand 65.82% and F1-Macro scores of 64.55% and 65.80% under oracle and inferred speaker regions, respectively.\n1096\tAcross both regression and classification tasks, the performance differences between oracle and inferred speaker\n1097\tregions remain relatively small. For example, the regression ensemble achieves nearly identical PCC and MAE values\n1098\tunder both conditions, while the classification ensemble exhibits slightly higher performance under inferred speaker\n1099\tregions. These findings suggest that the proposed speaker-role and language-diarization systems preserve the key\n1100\tconversational and language-use information required for downstream language proficiency prediction, making the\n1101\toverall pipeline practical for real-world deployment without requiring manual annotations.\n1102\tCompared with the regression task, the performance gains from ensembling are more pronounced for binary\n1103\tclassification. While the regression ensemble provides only marginal improvements over the Whisper-based system,\n1104\tthe classification ensemble improves F1-Macro by approximately 3–6 percentage points over the individual feature\n1105\tsets. This result suggests that the diarization-derived behavioral features and Whisper encoder representations provide\n1106\tcomplementary decision boundaries for separating lower- and higher-proficiency speakers. In contrast, the regression\n1107\ttask appears to be dominated by the Whisper representations, leaving less room for additional gains from behavioral\n1108\tfeature fusion.\n1109\t\n1110\t7. Conclusion\n1111\tIn this work, we have investigated automatic language proficiency assessment in multilingual interviewer–respondent\n1112\tinteractions among older adults. We have developed Whisper-based speaker role diarization and language diarization\n1113\tsystems to automatically extract respondent speech and characterize language usage patterns from conversational\n1114\trecordings. Using a multilingual subset of the LASI-DAD language proficiency assessment corpus, we have demonstrated that language-adapted Whisper models substantially improve language diarization performance, particularly\n1115\tfor lower-resource and linguistically related languages such as Marathi and Gujarati.\n1116\tBeyond diarization performance, we have shown that automatically derived conversational and language-use\n1117\tbehaviors provide meaningful and interpretable indicators of language proficiency. In particular, the respondent\n1118\tspeech ratio and intended-language usage showed strong, consistent positive associations with proficiency scores.\n1119\tFurthermore, simple diarization-derived behavioral features achieved performance comparable to Whisper-based\n1120\tspeech embeddings for proficiency prediction, while combining behavioral and speech representation features yielded\n1121\tthe strongest overall performance. Importantly, both the statistical relationships and prediction performance remained\n1122\tlargely preserved when using fully automatic speaker-role and language diarization outputs, demonstrating the\n1123\tfeasibility of scalable respondent-centric language proficiency assessment without manual annotations.\n1124\tFuture work can integrate ASR systems and speech LLMs into multilingual interview-based assessments to directly\n1125\tmodel lexical, grammatical, and semantic information in respondents’ speech. Combined with diarization-derived\n1126\tfeatures and Whisper encoder representations, these approaches could provide a more holistic assessment of proficiency\n1127\tby jointly leveraging acoustic, linguistic, and interactional cues while accommodating multilingual speech, codeswitching, and cross-lingual variation. Future studies could also compare speech behaviors when respondents use their\n1128\tfirst versus non-first languages and examine how language background affects the relationship between these behaviors\n1129\tand proficiency.\n1130\t\n1131\tAnfeng Xu et al: Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n1132\t\n1133\tPage 14 of 16\n1134\t\n1135\t\fSpeaker and Language Diarization\n1136\t\n1137\t8.\n1138\tCRediT authorship contribution statement\n1139\tAnfeng Xu: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Formal analysis, Investigation, Data curation, Validation,\n1140\tVisualization, Writing – original draft.. Tiantian Feng: Methodology, Data curation, Software, Writing – review\n1141\t& editing, . Kevin Huang: Investigation, Data curation, Writing – review & editing.. Pranali Khobragade: Data\n1142\tcuration, Resources, Writing – review & editing.. Sudarsana Kadiri: Data curation, Resources, Writing – review\n1143\t& editing.. Anushikha Dhankhar: Data curation, Project administration, Writing – review & editing.. Madeleine\n1144\tSnider: Data curation, Project administration, Writing – review & editing.. Sarah Gao: Project administration,\n1145\tResources, Writing – review & editing.. Miguel Arce Rentería: Supervision, Project administration, Resources,\n1146\tWriting – review & editing.. Jinkook Lee: Supervision, Project administration, Resources, Writing – review & editing..\n1147\tShrikanth Narayanan: Supervision, Project administration, Resources, Writing – review & editing..\n1148\t\n1149\tReferences\n1150\tBaevski, A., Zhou, Y., Mohamed, A., Auli, M., 2020. wav2vec 2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations. Advances\n1151\tin neural information processing systems 33, 12449–12460.\n1152\tBannò, S., Knill, K.M., Matassoni, M., Raina, V., Gales, M., 2023. Assessment of l2 oral proficiency using self-supervised speech representation\n1153\tlearning, ISCA.\n1154\tBannò, S., Matassoni, M., 2023. Proficiency assessment of l2 spoken english using wav2vec 2.0, in: 2022 IEEE Spoken Language Technology\n1155\tWorkshop (SLT), pp. 1088–1095. doi:10.1109/SLT54892.2023.10023019.\n1156\tChen, S., Wang, C., Chen, Z., Wu, Y., Liu, S., Chen, Z., Li, J., Kanda, N., Yoshioka, T., Xiao, X., et al., 2022. Wavlm: Large-scale self-supervised\n1157\tpre-training for full stack speech processing. IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Signal Processing 16, 1505–1518.\n1158\tChua, V.Y., Liu, H., Perera, L.P.G., Woon, F.T., Wong, J., Zhang, X., Khudanpur, S., Khong, A.W., Dauwels, J., Styles, S.J., 2023. Merlion\n1159\tccs challenge: A english-mandarin code-switching child-directed speech corpus for language identification and diarization. arXiv preprint\n1160\tarXiv:2305.18881 .\n1161\tEyben, F., Scherer, K.R., Schuller, B.W., Sundberg, J., André, E., Busso, C., Devillers, L.Y., Epps, J., Laukka